had essentially stopped all presidential duties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.680
He wasn't receiving the presidential daily&nbsp;
briefing. Even when he was receiving it. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.960
wasn't really paying attention, but he wasn't&nbsp;
even ostensibly receiving it. And then in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.160
period between the November 2020 election and&nbsp;
when Trump ultimately left the White House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.440
he wasn't doing any work. These people don't&nbsp;
actually want to work. They don't want to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.900
Madison Cawthorn has just left. He has abandoned&nbsp;
the job that, quite frankly, he was never actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.580
interested in doing. It was sort of like it&nbsp;
was sort of like college, right? He thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:29.340
this sounds kind of cool, but he didn't actually&nbsp;
want to do the stuff that's required. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.220
around him were a little bit creeped out. He did a&nbsp;
terrible job, messed up a bunch of stuff, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.320
ended up kind of going back home to live with his&nbsp;
family. They're not really sending their best. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.920
this is just another example where, again, I would&nbsp;
say to you, too, Republicans, are you so committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.060
to the party that you would quite literally&nbsp;
rather a guy who goes home instead of doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.680
